  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "For the function $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{\u2033}(1)>0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=-4{x}^{3}+4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{3}+4x & =0 \\\\ -4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -4x(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{\u2033}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(x)=-12{x}^{2}+4,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(-1) & =-8<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(0) & =4>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement IV is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"",
